\subsection{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:formulation}
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

\paragraph{Flow-matching action generation.} We use $t \in [0,1]$ exclusively for the flow-matching noise level and reserve index $j$ for the position within the action chunk of horizon $H$. $\pi_{0.5}$ generates robot action trajectories through conditional flow matching. Given an observation $o$ (RGB images and a language instruction), the model learns a velocity field $v_\theta(x_t, o, t)$ that transports samples from a noise distribution to the data distribution of action chunks. Let $a \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times D_a}$ denote a ground-truth action chunk of horizon $H$ and dimension $D_a$, and let $\epsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I)$ be Gaussian noise. The noisy interpolant at time $t \in [0, 1]$ is defined as
\begin{equation}
x_t = (1 - t) \, a + t \, \epsilon,
\end{equation}
where $t = 0$ corresponds to the clean action and $t = 1$ to pure noise. The conditional velocity field target is $u_t = \epsilon - a$, and the flow-matching objective minimizes
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FM}} = \mathbb{E}_{t, a, \epsilon} \left[ \left\| v_\theta(x_t, o, t) - (\epsilon - a) \right\|^2 \right].
\end{equation}
At inference, the model generates actions by integrating the learned velocity field from $t = 1$ (noise) to $t = 0$ (data) using an ODE solver.

\paragraph{Safety as collision avoidance.} We formalize safety through the signed distance field between the robot's end-effector and scene obstacles. For a robot configuration $q$ in a scene with obstacle set $\mathcal{O}$, the signed distance $s(q) = \min_{o \in \mathcal{O}} \mathrm{SDF}(q, o)$ is positive when the end-effector is separated from all obstacles and negative when penetration occurs. A trajectory is considered safe if $s(q_t) \geq \delta$ for all timesteps, where $\delta > 0$ is a safety margin. Our goal is to train a policy that intrinsically generates trajectories satisfying this constraint, without requiring runtime collision checking or trajectory filtering.

